//    most recently generated token is an explicit arithmetic cue such as "=",
//    "is", "are", "was", "were", "total", "sum", "plus", "minus", "times",
//    "left", or "remaining". This delays `<<` until a natural computation site.
// 2) Once inside a constrained span, keep spans short. If the constrained
//    prefix becomes complete, close immediately. If it grows beyond a small
//    budget, use rollback-to-boundary recovery and spend one step to avoid
//    unbounded malformed expressions.
// 3) Use adaptive/group-boosted constrained sampling only inside the span, and
//    penalize tokens from the provided groups outside the span so free-form text
//    is less likely to drift into symbolic fragments before `<<` opens.
// 4) After one constrained span has been closed, do not proactively open a new
//    one again; this avoids the repeated-open failure mode while still allowing
//    a single step-by-step computation when the model chooses an arithmetic cue.
//
// The goal is not to force every token into constrained mode, but to produce at
// least one well-formed short `<<...>>` computation at an appropriate location
// and then let the model finish normally.
